/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A thermal reservoir is a body so large that adding or removing heat does not change its temperature.
   - Every device in this chapter has the same three parts: a source, a sink, and a device placed between them.
   - Q₁ is the heat taken from the source, in kJ; W is the net work delivered by the device, in kJ; Q₂ is the heat given to the sink, in kJ.
   - The same balance serves engines and reversed devices.
   - A heat engine takes heat Q₁ from the source, converts part of it into work W, and rejects the rest Q₂ to the sink.
   - η (Greek eta) is the thermal efficiency, a pure fraction; multiply by 100 for per cent.
   - Efficiency is what you get divided by what you paid for.
   - Watch the denominator.
   - Reverse every arrow.
   - COPR = Q₂ / W and COPHP = Q₁ / W COP is the coefficient of performance, a pure number.
*/
