/* book exam traps: Kelvin-Planck is about a cyclic device, not one process. Isothermal expansion of an ideal gas is legal; repeating it needs compression work. | The statements are one law in two forms. A violation of one violates the other. | A PMM2 obeys conservation of energy. It breaks direction. Calling it a first-law violation is wrong; that is PMM1. | Clausius does not ban refrigerators. It bans heat uphill with no work and no other change. | For a reversible refrigerator, COP = T₂/(T₁ − T₂); cold temperature on top. */
